# Linux — OpenSCAP + SCAP Security Guide

---

## Tool

**OpenSCAP** (oscap command + SCAP Workbench GUI)
Open-source, free, used by enterprises worldwide.

---

## Installation

**RHEL / Rocky / Fedora / CentOS Stream:**
```bash
sudo dnf install openscap-scanner scap-security-guide
```
When to use: On Red Hat-based systems.

---

**Ubuntu / Debian:**
```bash
sudo apt install libopenscap8 ssg-base ssg-debderived
```
When to use: On Debian-based systems.

---

## Step-by-Step Process

**Step 1 — List available profiles/baselines:**
```bash
oscap info /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/ssg-rhel9-ds.xml
```
What it does: Shows all available security profiles (CIS, STIG, etc.) for your OS.
When to do this: To see which compliance profiles you can scan against.

---

**Step 2 — Run gap analysis (example using CIS profile):**
```bash
oscap xccdf eval --profile xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_cis \
    --results /tmp/results.xml --report /tmp/compliance-report.html \
    /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/ssg-rhel9-ds.xml
```
What it does: Scans your system against the CIS baseline and generates a report.
When to do this: During security audits or before putting a server in production.

---

**Step 3 — Open the report:**
```bash
firefox /tmp/compliance-report.html
```
What it shows: PASS/FAIL list with detailed explanations, severity, and remediation steps.
When to check: After every scan to see what needs fixing.

---

**Step 4 — Generate automatic remediation script (optional):**
```bash
oscap xccdf generate fix --profile xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_cis \
    --output /tmp/remediation.sh /tmp/results.xml
```
What it does: Creates a bash script that automatically fixes the failed checks.
When to do this: When you want to auto-fix gaps instead of doing it manually.

---

**Step 5 — Review and run the fix script:**
```bash
cat /tmp/remediation.sh    # Review first!
sudo bash /tmp/remediation.sh
```
What it does: Applies the security fixes to your system.
When to do this: After reviewing the script — never run blindly in production.

---

**Step 6 — Re-scan to confirm compliance:**
```bash
# Run the same oscap eval command from Step 2 again
```
When to do this: Always after applying fixes to verify they worked.

